### 2. OS Internals, Kernel & Hardware Architecture -- Max: 25 points
Assess demonstrated knowledge of what happens below userspace. A systems engineer must understand how the OS manages resources beneath the application layer AND how the hardware executes their code.

**OPERATING SYSTEM INTERNALS (up to 13 points):**

HIGH (10-13):
- Kernel module or device driver development (Linux, Windows WDF, or RTOS)
- Writing or modifying bootloaders, UEFI applications; understanding BIOS/UEFI boot chain and kernel initialization
- Custom filesystem implementation or block device drivers
- Network stack internals (custom TCP/IP, XDP, DPDK userspace networking)
- eBPF programs for tracing, networking, or security
- Hypervisor, VMM, or virtualization work (KVM, Xen, custom VMM)
- Memory management internals: virtual memory, paging, page faults, heap vs. stack, memory fragmentation, custom malloc implementations, MMU, page tables, TLB
- Concurrency internals: kernel threads vs. user threads, context switching cost, mutexes, spinlocks, condition variables, lock-free data structures, futex
- Process/thread lifecycle: scheduling algorithms, IPC (pipes, shared memory, message queues, Unix domain sockets), signals
- Container internals (namespaces, cgroups, seccomp) -- NOT just using Docker

MEDIUM (5-9):
- Linux systems programming with system calls (epoll, io_uring, mmap, futex)
- POSIX API expertise (pthreads, signals, shared memory, semaphores)
- Experience with real-time operating systems (FreeRTOS, Zephyr, QNX)
- Cross-compilation and embedded toolchains
- Basic understanding of scheduling, IPC, virtual memory concepts
- Socket programming with understanding of TCP/IP internals (not just calling send/recv)

LOW (1-4):
- Basic multithreading with pthreads or std::thread
- Socket programming at the application level only
- Academic OS coursework (xv6, PintOS) without extensions or original work
- Using Linux as a development environment without systems-level work

**HARDWARE ARCHITECTURE & MICROARCHITECTURE (up to 12 points):**

HIGH (9-12):
- CPU architecture: instruction pipelining, branch prediction, out-of-order execution, superscalar execution
- Memory hierarchy mastery: L1/L2/L3 caches, cache lines (typically 64 bytes), cache coherence protocols (MESI/MOESI), false sharing, and ability to write cache-friendly code
- Peripherals & buses: understanding of PCI/PCIe, I2C, SPI, USB, and how the CPU communicates with peripherals
- Direct Memory Access (DMA): how hardware reads/writes memory independently of the CPU, scatter-gather DMA
- Bare-metal embedded firmware (ARM Cortex-M/A, RISC-V)
- FPGA or custom silicon experience
- NUMA awareness for multi-socket systems

MEDIUM (4-8):
- Basic understanding of CPU pipeline, branch prediction, cache hierarchy
- Awareness of cache effects on performance (AoS vs SoA)
- Embedded development with microcontrollers
- Understanding of memory-mapped I/O

LOW (1-3):
- Knows caches exist but no evidence of applying knowledge
- Basic Arduino/Raspberry Pi projects without hardware-level understanding

**CRITICAL RULES:**
- "Linux" listed as a skill without kernel/systems evidence scores 0 points in this category
- Using Docker/Kubernetes is DevOps, NOT OS internals -- score 0-2 points maximum
- Academic OS coursework (xv6, PintOS) without extensions or original work scores 3-5 points total
- Understanding "why epoll exists" (event-driven I/O vs thread-per-connection tradeoffs) matters more than just calling epoll

